dent of ordinary replication. This is an applicability gap for a stronger obligation, not proof that no such requirement exists elsewhere.\",\n      \"A same-history run with legal network delays and continuing heartbeat responses while ordinary replication is blocked has not been executed. Network fast-path dispatch is distinct from consumeCh dispatch, but code separation alone establishes no duration or reachable deployment endpoint.\",\n      \"No bound on stepDown notification consumption is established under main-thread blocking or arbitrary scheduling.\",\n      \"Already-completed verification, concurrent replies, configuration change, and stale-read/commit consequences have not been checked.\",\n      \"Transport timeouts, connection allocation, and receiver storage blocking remain unexpanded.\",\n      \"No claim about commits, linearizable reads, or deployment outcomes is supported.\"\n    ],\n    \"understanding\": \"unchanged\",\n    \"rationale\": \"The accepted map already gives the principal fact only error-free heartbeat-contact meaning and maps its timestamp consumer. Additional reads explain candidate-specific protections without changing any fact meaning or edge, so no new map version is warranted. Next research decision: examine the separately sourced VerifyLeader support relation and its request/reply correlation before proposing a distinct question; do not carry forward an assumed stale-read consequence.\"\n  },\n  \"counterevidence_resolution\": \"No previous counterevidence was removed: all three accepted protection statements remain verbatim. The prior unknowns were refined, not silently discharged. (1) The prompt-step-down contract question remains open beyond the inspected lease interface; s-lease-contract establishes only loss-of-contact semantics. (2) Sustained heartbeat replies with blocked ordinary replication remain an unexecuted history; s-heartbeat-dispatch-shape and s-heartbeat-dispatch establish distinct dispatch routes only. (3) The compound unknown about stepDown consumption, worker lifetime and transport behavior is partially answered: s-stepdown-consume shows receipt transitions to Follower; s-replication-loop shows replicate exit closes stopHeartbeat and CommitTimeout initiates ordinary attempts. s-heartbeat-dispatch and s-heartbeat-handler trace the fast-path call. These observations do not resolve transport timeout/connection semantics, scheduling bounds, or completion of in-flight RPCs; those remain explicit unknowns. (4) The exclusion of commit, linearizable-read and deployment claims remains in the rationale, importance and refined unknowns. The scoped explanation resolves only suspected strengthening of contact at the lease consumer, not these broader questions.\"\n}\nACCEPTED QUESTION {\n  \"audit_spec_version\": 1,\n  \"disposition\": \"concrete_suspicion\",\n  \"preferred_check\": \"source_review\",\n  \"requests\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"raft.go\",\n      \"start_line\": 680,\n      \"end_line\": 706,\n      \"reason\": \"Trace completion of the stale-term stepDown notification.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"net_transport.go\",\n      \"symbol\": \"handleCommand\",\n      \"reason\": \"Determine heartbeat fast-path selection and blocking isolation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"replication.go\",\n      \"start_line\": 137,\n      \"end_line\": 200,\n      \"reason\": \"Determine whether routine retries bound the lifetime of the suspected interval.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"question\": \"When a heartbeat receives an error-free rejection carrying a newer term while ordinary replication is delayed, what authority meaning, if any, may the lease consumer assign to the refreshed contact timestamp, and which independent mechanism forces step-down?\",\n  \"importance\": \"Potential continued leader status under stale authority could affect availability or caller interpretation of leadership. Neither divergent decisions nor stale reads have been established; those require separate endpoint evidence.\",\n  \"source_ids\": [\n    \"s-constructor\",\n    \"s-heartbeat\",\n    \"s-repl-context\",\n    \"s-spawn\",\n    \"s-append-response\",\n    \"s-pipeline-response\",\n    \"s-stale\",\n    \"s-lease\",\n    \"s-lease-contract\",\n    \"s-receiver\",\n    \"s-verify\",\n    \"s-verify-result\"\n  ],\n  \"participants\": [\n    \"Local leader worker\",\n    \"Remote peer with a newer current term\"\n  ],\n  \"objects\": [\n    \"followerReplication.lastContact\",\n    \"AppendEntries response\",\n    \"LeaderLeaseTimeout\"\n  ],\n  \"contexts\": [\n    \"One replication object and its fixed request term\",\n    \"Latest configuration voter quorum\",\n    \"Crash-only message delay; no forged peer responses\"\n  ],\n  \"event_paths\": [\n    \"Older-term appendEntries rejection -> error-free heartbeat response -> setLastContact -> checkLeaderLease\",\n    \"Ordinary/pipelined newer-term response -> handleStaleTerm -> stepDown notification\",\n    \"Heartbeat rejection -> notifyAll(false) -> pending VerifyLeader processing\"\n  ],\n  \"activity_classes\": [\n    \"A2\"\n  ],\n  \"behavior_ids\": [\n    \"b-heartbeat-contact\",\n    \"b-lease-consume\"\n  ],\n  \"fact_ids\": [\n    \"f-heartbeat-contact\"\n  ],\n  \"obligation_relation_kind\": \"consumption\",\n  \"counterevidence\": [\n    \"The LeaderLeaseTimeout interface promises step-down without contact, not without same-term approval; an error-free rejection is still contact.\",\n    \"Ordinary and pipelined replication call handleStaleTerm on newer response terms.\",\n    \"Heartbeat passes resp.Success to verify futures, and a negative verification can transition the main thread to Follower.\"\n  ],\n  \"unknowns\": [\n    \"Whether any applicable interface promises prompt step-down after this heartbeat response independent of ordinary replication.\",\n    \"Whether legal transport scheduling permits sustained heartbeat replies while ordinary replication cannot expose the newer term.\",\n    \"Exact stepDown consumption, worker lifetime and transport timeout/fast-path behavior.\",\n    \"No claim about commits, linearizable reads, or deployment outcomes is supported.\"\n  ],\n  \"priority\": 2,\n  \"trigger_rationale\": \"Cross-path comparison found different response-term handling on independent workers updating shared contact evidence. The actual consumer checks only elapsed contact time; contract applicability and alternate step-down paths must be resolved before an obligation.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
